    "text": "**Case A**\n\n- **A1 \u2013 Cannot tell.** The search covered only the past seven days, so it cannot rule out earlier misuse outside that window, and the burst itself is not yet confirmed as misuse.\n- **A2 \u2013 Yes.** The burst and the account changes came at 19 minutes and the check scored the account at 30 minutes, so the evidence already existed when it ran. This assumes the check could see request and account-change activity, which the case does not state.\n- **A3 \u2013 No, not by itself.** A shared payout destination is a link worth following, but it could equally be a mule destination or a shared processor, not common ownership.\n- **A4 \u2013 Yes, weakly.** Thirty days without a report is mild evidence for legitimacy, since a diverted payout would usually be noticed, but reports can lag and nobody may have looked.\n- **A5 \u2013 Yes.** A new network origin and fingerprint minutes after publication, combined with a payout destination change and a faster schedule, clearly warrants investigation.\n\n**Case B**\n\n- **B1 \u2013 Yes.** With 100 completed transactions each, D's recorded rate is 0 percent against 2 percent for the others, though the difference is small and may not be meaningful.\n- **B2 \u2013 No.** A combined figure is an average across the four accounts; one account can sit above 5 percent while others sit below.\n- **B3 \u2013 Yes.** The search covered all merchant accounts and found none beyond A and the closed account, so on the records there are no others in that person's name. It cannot exclude accounts held through nominees.\n- **B4 \u2013 Cannot tell.** Different analysts on different days does not establish independence; both may have worked from the same model output and rejected application, or the second may have read the first.\n- **B5 \u2013 No.** A blocked payment never completed, so it could not generate a chargeback; the absence of one says nothing about whether it was fraud.\n- **B6 \u2013 Yes.** Shared templates,